\section{Limitations}

All results use the DocRED development set; test set submission is pending. Exact-match F1 penalizes surface-form-different entity mentions~\citep{wan2024genres}, likely underestimating absolute extraction capability, though relative comparisons remain valid.

We evaluate one algorithm per RL family (GRPO, DPO); other algorithms may yield different results. GRPO uses a single KL coefficient (0.01) and DPO a single $\beta$ (0.1); alternative hyperparameter settings may produce different decoupling behaviors and warrant future exploration. CED scoring depends on an external NLI model, and the RSFT ablation uses limited seeds (four for CED, two for flat-NLI). The evidence tax pattern observed up to 8B may not extrapolate to larger scales. At 1.7B scale, 44.4\% of outputs exhibit repetitive patterns (vs.\ negligible at 4B+); small models may require decoding-time interventions (e.g., repetition penalty) for reliable deployment.

\section{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available benchmarks (DocRED, Re-DocRED) derived from Wikipedia, containing no private or sensitive information. All models are trained on existing annotated data without human subjects involvement. Our evidence-grounding approach aims to improve the verifiability of relation extraction outputs, which supports responsible deployment in high-stakes domains such as clinical and legal information systems by enabling human verification of model predictions. We release code and model outputs to support reproducibility.

\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\paragraph{A. Did you describe the limitations of your work?}
Yes. Section ``Limitations'' discusses evaluation on development set only, limited RL algorithm coverage, seed count, scale extrapolation, and 1.7B repetition issues.

\paragraph{B. Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}
Yes. The Ethics Statement discusses data sources, absence of human subjects, and the intended use for improving verifiability in high-stakes domains.

\paragraph{C. Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}
Yes. The abstract states three findings (decoupling spectrum, scale-dependent evidence tax, RSFT effectiveness) with supporting numbers. The introduction provides detailed motivation, findings, and a contribution list.

\paragraph{D1. Did you include the code, data, and instructions needed to reproduce the main experimental results?}
We will release training and evaluation code upon publication. All experiments use publicly available datasets (DocRED, Re-DocRED) and base models (Qwen3, LLaMA-3.1) accessible through standard channels.

\paragraph{D2. Did you specify all the training and test details?}
Yes. Appendix Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams} provides complete hyperparameters for all methods. Section~\ref{sec:setup} describes datasets, models, and metrics. We report four seeds for the primary configuration and standard deviations where applicable.

\paragraph{D3. Did you report error bars or statistical significance?}
Partially. We report standard deviations for the primary RSFT configuration (four seeds) and per-seed breakdowns in the appendix. Limited seed counts for some configurations (two for flat-NLI, one for 1.7B/8B) are acknowledged as limitations.

\paragraph{D4. Did you use existing packages for known algorithms?}
Yes. We use HuggingFace Transformers, TRL, and PEFT for model training. The NLI model for CED scoring is a publicly available cross-encoder checkpoint.

\paragraph{E. Did you use human annotators?}
No. All experiments use existing annotated benchmarks.

\paragraph{F. Did you use AI assistants?}
Yes. AI assistants were used for code development and paper drafting. All scientific claims, experimental design, and analysis are the responsibility of the authors.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
We conduct experiments on DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred}, a large-scale document-level relation extraction benchmark containing 3,053 training and 998 development documents spanning 96 relation types. Each document requires identifying relational triples across multiple sentences and grounding each triple with supporting evidence sentences. We use Re-DocRED~\citep{tan2022redocred} for cross-dataset generalization evaluation.

\paragraph{Models.}
Our primary model is Qwen3-4B. To study scale effects, we additionally train Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B under identical configurations. We include LLaMA-3.1-8B as a cross-family validation point.

\paragraph{Training.}
All models use LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} with rank $r{=}64$ and $\alpha{=}128$ over a structured output format that jointly produces relation triples and evidence sentences. For RSFT, we sample $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per document and select the top-$k{=}1$ response ranked by CED scoring (\S\ref{sec:ced}), which combines NLI-based evidence faithfulness with extraction accuracy. GRPO experiments use group sizes $G{=}4$ (QLoRA, 4-bit) and $G{=}8$ (bf16 full-precision LoRA). DPO constructs preference pairs from CED-ranked outputs.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
We report five metrics. \textbf{Ign-F1} is the primary DocRED metric that ignores triples appearing in the training set, matching the official leaderboard protocol. \textbf{Rel-F1} measures relation extraction F1 on all triples. \textbf{Evi-F1 (TP-only)} measures evidence sentence F1 conditioned on correctly predicted triples, reflecting evidence quality independent of extraction errors. \textbf{Evi-F1 (joint)} includes false-positive and false-negative triples in the denominator following the DREEAM protocol~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, enabling cross-method evidence comparison. \textbf{EDCR} (Evidence-Decoupling Ratio) is our proposed diagnostic: the ratio of evidence recall on correctly-predicted triples to overall extraction recall, where values substantially exceeding the SFT baseline indicate RL-induced decoupling. All primary results use the development set; test set submission is pending.


\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison between SFT, no-evidence SFT, and RSFT CED on Qwen3-4B. The SFT baseline achieves 39.74 Ign-F1 with joint evidence generation. Removing evidence from the output format entirely (no-evidence SFT) improves extraction to 47.21 Ign-F1, a 7.47-point gap that quantifies the \emph{evidence tax} at the 4B scale. RSFT CED achieves a four-seed mean of 44.66 Ign-F1 (+4.92 over SFT, standard deviation 0.49) while maintaining evidence quality (Evi-F1 81.15 vs.\ 79.86 for SFT). The EDCR decreases from 0.6982 to 0.6503, confirming tighter coupling between extraction and evidence capabilities. Per-seed results appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:ablation_full}.

The remaining gap to the no-evidence upper bound (2.09 points) represents the residual cost of producing structured evidence, a cost that shrinks at larger scales as we show in Section~\ref{sec:scaling}.

